<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ine of Wessex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 670–c. 728</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> was king of the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> from 688 to 726, one of the strongest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> rulers before Alfred. He issued an important law code, founded or endowed churches including Glastonbury, and pushed West Saxon power westward into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, fighting King Geraint of the British kingdom of Dumnonia around 710. He abdicated in 726 and went on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex.jpg" alt="Ine of Wessex" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Greenshed · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Ine_in_the_Transfiguration_Window_of_Wells_Cathedral_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ine was born around 670, the son of Cenred and grandson of Ceolwald. His brother Ingild was named in the royal genealogies as the ancestor of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Egbert of Wessex</a> and the later kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. His sister Cuthburh married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldfrith-of-northumbria">Aldfrith of Northumbria</a>, and Ine himself married Æthelburg. Bede calls him 'of the blood royal', meaning the royal line of the Gewisse, the early West Saxon tribe. He came to the throne after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caedwalla">Cædwalla</a> abdicated and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to be baptised, and there is some uncertainty about the transition. He may have ruled for a time alongside his father, who was still reigning according to a surviving land grant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ine's predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caedwalla">Cædwalla</a> had made himself overlord of most of the southern kingdoms, but by the end of Ine's reign Kent, Sussex, Surrey and Essex had slipped out of West Saxon control. Ine made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wihtred-of-kent">Wihtred of Kent</a> in 694, when Wihtred paid compensation for the death of Cædwalla's brother Mul. He kept control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a> and what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, and in 710 he fought with Nothhelm of Sussex against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geraint-of-dumnonia">Geraint of Dumnonia</a>, whom John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> says was killed. In 721 he killed one Cynewulf, and in 722 his queen destroyed Taunton, which he had built around 710. In 722 he invaded Sussex because of the exile Ealdberht, and three years later he invaded again and killed him.</p><p>His code of laws, issued in 694 or shortly before, was the first by an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> king outside Kent and survives because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> appended it to his own. It addresses baptism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithes</a> and church dues as well as civil matters such as straying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, giving the earliest documentary evidence of open-field farming. It sets fines for neglecting military service and gave Britons half the weregild of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> of the same rank. Ealdormen and shires are first mentioned in his reign. The trading settlement of Hamwic, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a>, grew to a population estimated at 5,000. Ine patronised religious houses and, at Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldhelm">Aldhelm</a>'s suggestion in 705, built the church that later became Wells Cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 726 Ine abdicated, with no obvious heir, and left the kingdom, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s words, to younger men. He travelled with Æthelburg to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where both died. Roger of Wendover says that Ine founded the Schola Saxonum there in 727, though tradition also gives that role to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa of Mercia</a>. His successor Æthelheard faced a rival claim from the ætheling Oswald.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ine of Wessex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ine</b>’s laws give Britons lower wergilds than Englishmen, showing that a large British population lived under West Saxon rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings are poorly recorded; Geraint fought <b>Ine of Wessex</b> around 710.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cornwall">Kingdom of Cornwall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 710 <b>Ine of Wessex</b> fought Geraint, and Devon was gradually absorbed.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dumnonia">Kingdom of Dumnonia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=707" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Ine of Wessex"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=707">What was here in 707 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
